+## Native temporal frames supplied by a launcher
+
+The native executable registers an optional generic media reader when
+`FILMCRAFT_FRAMES_PORT` and `FILMCRAFT_FRAMES_TOKEN` are set. The port must be
+nonzero and the token must contain 64 hexadecimal characters. The destination
+is always IPv4 loopback. A source file contains exactly
+`FilmCraft Native Frames 1\n` followed by a 32-character hexadecimal source id.
+Files and saved projects contain neither the port nor the token.
+
+Each TCP connection carries one JSON line:
+`{"token":"…","source":"…","method":"describe"}` returns
+`{"ok":true,"info":MediaInfo}`. The description must specify RGBA8, square
+pixels, no audio, positive duration up to the largest safe JSON integer, at most
+8192 pixels per side and 33,554,432 total pixels. Frame-rate numerator/denominator
+are bounded to 120000/10000.
+
+`{"token":"…","source":"…","method":"frame","time":"<ticks>"}` returns
+`{"ok":true,"time":"<same ticks>","bytes":<width*height*4>}` and then exactly
+that many raw straight RGBA8 bytes. Time is in FilmCraft media ticks, in
+`[0,duration)`. Errors use `{"ok":false,"error":"…"}`. Reply headers are limited
+to 16 KiB, connections to two seconds and responses to thirty seconds.
+Hosts with a full request queue reply `{"ok":false,"busy":true}`. The reader
+retries after 50 ms within the original deadline; other errors are not retried.
+Reads poll the existing cancellation token and preserve cancellation during both
+header and pixel reads as `MediaError::Cancelled`. Superseded playback requests
+therefore do not count as damaged media; the next request can retry normally.
+Bad times, lengths and descriptions
+fail before pixel allocation. Preview, thumbnails and export use the existing
+native media interface and caches.
+Playback of projects containing `.fcframes` sources finishes one coherent
+picture before catching up to the clock. This avoids starving a slow host by
+continually cancelling its current render. Paused seeks and exports still ask
+for the exact requested time.
+
+The host owns source identity, authorization, persistence and re-registration.
+Reopening a project in another launch requires its host to reconstruct the
+sources and replace the temporary file paths before opening it in FilmCraft.
+The native reader has no application-specific recipe or reference schema.
